module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_signal_target_set_chosen_by_name_on_a_shared_uid_kills_other_sessions_work

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyImpossible }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// THE ACTUATION-SIDE CLASS OF SHARED-HOST SIGNAL TARGETING: a worker chooses the SUBJECT of a
// signal by matching text or identity (a name, a pattern, a uid) instead of signalling a pid it
// RECORDED for itself after an ownership check. On a uid shared by many concurrent sessions the
// match's extension is everyone's population, so one session's cleanup is another session's voided
// run. This is the class recorded three times on srv1 on 2026-11-26 alone. The row is the record;
// it is not a rung claim beyond what the receipts below state.

data a_signal_target_set_chosen_by_name_on_a_shared_uid_kills_other_sessions_work: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_signal_target_set_chosen_by_name_on_a_shared_uid_kills_other_sessions_work" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, ceiling: StructurallyImpossible, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "the capability of a per-pid host kill route that verifies ownership before signalling -- slot .who names the acquirer, the target's cgroup shows the acquirer's heavy-slot scope, readlink exe and cwd fall inside the acquirer's own scratch, the target's log path is the acquirer's own, all four re-read immediately before the signal (the four-way check; reading any pid's environ on srv1 is FORBIDDEN, Manager B 2026-11-26) -- delivered as a srv1 host change through the deploy route with operator approval" } },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: kill, pkill or killall targets are chosen by MATCHING a name, pattern, or uid (pkill -f, pkill -u -x, a kill fed by pgrep) on a uid shared by many sessions, instead of a recorded own pid that passed an ownership check. The matcher's extension is the whole uid population; the caller's population is only the pids it launched and recorded.",
    "HARM: other sessions' runs are VOIDED mid-flight and their heavy slots wasted, and the deaths read as UNEXPLAINED, triggering incident work including false hunts: on 2026-11-26 Manager A briefly read the 18:34Z death cluster as a user-systemd event before the pattern kill was found, and Manager B opened a host-wide sweep probe and put a wet-spawn launch hold across lanes on the same unexplained deaths.",
    "SPECIMENS, three recorded. (1) valiant-badger-417: pkill -f heavy-gate at about 13:51:40, 13:54:53 and 13:58:20Z on 2026-11-26 (SIGTERM; the pattern matched the caller's own heavy-gate) -- then kill -9 over the pids of pgrep -x gunbc, 19 pids, at 14:00:11-14Z. Times and commands per Manager A's message msg fc9c117e, relayed by Manager B in msg_0b1d913d; quoted at command-string grain, not as anyone's prose. (2) bold-cat-668: pkill -u briansrls -x gunbc on srv1; the true kill window is 2026-11-26T18:34:22.5Z to 18:34:25.2Z per his correction msg_d427cea8, which supersedes his first self-report of 18:35:11-14Z; victim runs cluster at 18:34:27-31Z per the tally in msg fc9c117e (about 18:34:30Z, warm-fox-517 pinning exact times). One named victim: session clever-carp-758's roadmap_frontier_witness leg, pid 2592719, inside the shared limiter under scope heavy-slot-2591745.scope per the journal, rc 143, killed while Manager B's sweep-probe hold was live -- the voided run also forfeited a completed identity leg that had to be re-run. (3) the 2026-11-20 pgrep -x claim_batch sweep already recorded in gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses (FOUND: 2026-11-20, around 01:20Z, srv1 PIDs 1821238 and 1821317 with SIGKILL): cited here as a specimen of THIS class; that row carries the downstream lease-charge consequence, which is not the class of choosing the set.",
    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::observer_inside_its_own_observed_population is about OBSERVATION -- a matcher whose extension contains its own evaluator while READING; this row is about ACTUATION -- signalling a set chosen by name, pattern, or uid, wider than the caller's own recorded processes. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses is the DOWNSTREAM CONSEQUENCE of one such kill (a serve process dying on a lease charge), and does not cover the class of target selection itself. And an audit of this class by grep OVER-REPORTS, the same way the 2026-11-26 environ audit did (21 hits, 0 actual reads -- rule text and audit tooling in the log): counting violations must separate naming or matching text from EXECUTING a signal; the three specimens above are executed kills with victim runs and journal entries, not grep artifacts (Manager B, msg_ed3d792f).",
    "PROPAGATION NOTE (Manager B, msg_ed3d792f): an ownership recipe must be COPIED from the authority text, never paraphrased -- paraphrase is how a retired HEAVY_OWNER-in-environ ownership clause survived in lane footers copied from charter text and was faithfully executed by workers in three lanes (seven count-only reads on 2026-11-26, all disclosed, none committed). That failure is OBSERVATION-side propagation of procedure text; it is distinct in mechanism from this row's actuation-side target-set choice, so it is named here as a corroborating fact and RECOMMENDED for its own row by the procedure-governance owner rather than folded into this class, where a merged ceiling would confuse a copy-discipline trigger with a signal-gating trigger.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigation by prose rule -- the fleet carries never-pattern-kill-on-a-shared-host as a discipline, and workers follow it; nothing refuses it mechanically, and on 2026-11-26 the prose had been newly written and two pattern kills still landed the same day. CEILING, stated as the capability: structurally impossible while the only host kill route verifies per-pid ownership before signalling. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS A CAPABILITY, two halves: (a) a per-pid host kill tool that verifies ownership by the four-way check spelled once in this row's trigger_capability -- delivered as a srv1 host change through the deploy route with operator approval, admitted the way gunbc.live_deploy.pre_swap_admission::observe_serve_entry_compile_clean and gunbc.live_deploy.pre_swap_admission::observe_served_command_reaches_bind admit a served build; and (b) eager-koi-371's candidate: heavy-slot refuses to RELEASE a slot whose scope still contains a process the acquirer did not record, so stray-hunting by name is never needed. ONLY half (a) removes the invalid state's constructor: while any unnamed-target kill route exists, the invalid state is writable, and half (b) alone does not refuse pkill of another session's processes -- it removes the NEED to hunt strays by name, so it is complementary, not sufficient. The ceiling claim is therefore stated for (a); (b) is named because without it a per-pid route still leaves lanes hunting strays by name after a refused release. Both are unbuilt, which is why the standing is BelowCeiling with blocker ClimbableButUnbuilt.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses", decl_name: "a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.observer_inside_its_own_observed_population", decl_name: "observer_inside_its_own_observed_population"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.live_deploy.pre_swap_admission", decl_name: "observe_serve_entry_compile_clean"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.live_deploy.pre_swap_admission", decl_name: "observe_served_command_reaches_bind"),
  ],
}
